  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Hawaii totaled $2.7B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $2.1B of that total. Federal agencies awarded 22,899 contracts to Hawaii-based and out-of-state vendors, with an average award size of $117K. Obligations fell 51.5% year over year. Totals are partial (fiscal year to date). Historical context shows higher annual obligations in recent years, with FY24 at $6.7B and FY25 at $5.5B. Construction and engineering industries account for a large share of current-year obligations, led by Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $603M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $589M. Major vendors include NAN INC with $388M and DRAGADOS/HAWAIIAN DREDGING/ORION JV with $355M. This snapshot highlights concentration in defense spending and large infrastructure contracts in the current fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How does FY2026 federal spending in Hawaii compare with prior years?",
        "body": "FY26 obligations are $2.7B. FY25 obligations were $5.5B and FY24 obligations were $6.7B. FY23 obligations were $4.4B, FY22 obligations were $3.5B, and FY21 obligations were $2.8B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate FY2026 federal obligations in Hawaii?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer with $2.1B in obligations. The General Services Administration obligated $352M and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $127M. Other leading industries by obligation are Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $603M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $589M."
      }
    ]
  },
